sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant R as Reseller server
    participant API as GET /merchant/v1/orders/{merchant_order_id}
    participant S as merchants.order_status.read
    participant O as orders.service.find_merchant_order
    participant F as fulfillment.service
    participant ST as fulfillment.stall.order_is_stalled
    participant L as merchants.deposit.refunded_for_order
    participant DB as Postgres

    R->>API: GET /merchant/v1/orders/acme%2F2026%2F000417 (signed)
    Note over R,API: merchant_order_id is ^[!-~]+$, which INCLUDES "/" -- the id<br/>travels percent-encoded and the route uses Starlette's :path convertor
    API->>API: merchant_auth verifies HMAC over the RAW request line ("%2F" as sent)
    Note over API: sign the bytes, match the value -- the two must not be confused
    API->>S: read(merchant=<from the signature>, merchant_order_id="acme/2026/000417")

    S->>O: find_merchant_order(merchant_id, merchant_order_id)
    Note over S,O: scope is the AUTHENTICATED merchant; no parameter names one
    O->>DB: SELECT orders WHERE idempotency_key = ? AND merchant_id = ?<br/>+ items + events (eager)
    alt no such order, or it belongs to another merchant
        S-->>R: 404 order_not_found -- one identical body for both,<br/>so "not yours" is not an oracle for a competitor's numbering
    end

    S->>L: refunded_for_order(merchant_id, order_id) + charged_for_order(...)
    L->>DB: SUM(legs on merchant_deposit) WHERE reference = (order, id),<br/>one query per direction
    Note over L: computed, never stored -- which is why M3b's automatic refund<br/>and an operator's attributed settlement both land here with no change<br/>to this read. The same sum picks failure_reason, compared against what<br/>the order was CHARGED and not against zero: complete =&gt;<br/>fulfillment_failed_refunded, a partial settlement =&gt; fulfillment_failed

    S->>ST: order_is_stalled(order)
    alt every item is delivered / failed / refunded
        ST-->>S: False -- no query at all, the answer cannot be yes
    else some item is still open
        ST->>DB: SELECT 1 FROM fulfillment_tasks JOIN order_items<br/>WHERE order_id = ? AND task.status = 'failed'<br/>AND item.fulfillment_state IN (pending, reserved, in_progress)
        Note over ST,DB: one indexed read on ix_fulfillment_tasks_order_id (0008). DERIVED,<br/>never stored: every route out of a stall already moves the task or the<br/>item, so there is no marker any site can forget to clear
        ST-->>S: stalled?
    end

    S->>S: failure_reason, in this precedence:<br/>order.status == failed -&gt; "order_failed"<br/>else any item failed -&gt; "fulfillment_failed[_refunded]"<br/>else stalled -&gt; "fulfillment_delayed" (NON-TERMINAL)<br/>else null
    Note over S: the terminal values come FIRST: a closed or failed order is over<br/>whatever its task is still doing. It reads the ITEM, not the task, because<br/>a supplier failure for OUR low balance leaves the item in_progress on<br/>purpose -- a reseller must not refund their customer for an order we will<br/>deliver. What it never says is WHY it is delayed: that we are short of<br/>balance at a named supplier is our supply, not their order

    S->>F: list_deliveries_for_order(order_id)
    F->>DB: SELECT deliveries JOIN order_items
    S->>F: buyer_safe_artifact(delivery)
    Note over S,F: the storefront's own allow-list, shared not copied:<br/>the voucher code goes out, the supplier's name and order id do not

    S-->>R: 200 {status, price_usd, refunded_usd, failure_reason,<br/>delivery{artifact_kind, artifact}, timeline[], ...}
    Note over R: THE ONLY place the voucher code is handed over --<br/>M3's webhook will not carry it (a bearer instrument in two log files)
